 **Parameters:**
-*   **Isovalue:** The scalar threshold for surface extraction. Typical values depend on the specific dataset [G].
-*   **MRT Count:** Fixed at 4 targets to store up to 4 vertices per tetrahedron [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Isovalue:** The scalar threshold for extraction.
+*   **MRT Count:** Fixed at 4 to accommodate the maximum number of intersections per tetrahedron [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Linear interpolation assumes linear scalar variation within the tetrahedron, which may not hold for complex fields [G].
-*   **Memory:** The technique is limited by the amount of video memory available for the MRTs and texture storage [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **CPU-GPU Transfer:** Performance is impacted by the speed of CPU-GPU memory transfers if data needs to be moved between host and device [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Limitations:** The technique is limited by the amount of video memory available on the graphics card, as it stores mesh data and MRTs in GPU memory [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **CPU-GPU Transfer Speed:** Performance is impacted by the speed of CPU-GPU memory transfers if data needs to be updated frequently [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Cases:** If a vertex lies exactly on the isovalue, special handling is required to avoid ambiguity in edge intersection calculation [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Rasterization-based Isosurface Extraction (Alpha-Test/Stencil)
-An early technique that computes plane-tetrahedra intersections using rasterization hardware with alpha-test and stencil buffer functionality, without programmable shaders [S1, unofficial]. It draws the back and front faces of a given cell in order, with the interpolated scalar value stored at the alpha component [S1, unofficial]. The alpha-test is configured to reject fragments less than the desired isovalue [S1, unofficial]. The combination of results from back and front faces is obtained using a XOR operation on the stencil buffer [S1, unofficial]. This algorithm runs in two passes (the first sets the stencil buffer) and renders an average of 5 to 7 triangles per tetrahedron [S1, unofficial].
+An early technique that computes plane-tetrahedra intersections using fixed-function rasterization hardware with alpha-test and stencil buffers, without programmable shaders [S1, unofficial]. It draws the back and front faces of a cell in order, storing an interpolated scalar value in the alpha component, and configures the alpha-test to reject fragments below the isovalue [S1, unofficial]. The final intersection is obtained by performing a XOR operation on the stencil buffer between the back and front face passes [S1, unofficial]. This method runs in two passes and renders an average of 5 to 7 triangles per cell [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of tetrahedra, as each tetrahedron is processed once [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of tetrahedra and the size of the MRTs and texture storage [G].
-*   **Performance:** The method is efficient in leveraging GPU parallelism but is limited by video memory capacity and CPU-GPU transfer speeds [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Allows for view-independent extraction and flexible post-processing via render-to-vertex-arrays [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage due to MRTs and texture storage; potential bottlenecks in CPU-GPU data transfer [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When flexible post-processing or view-independent extraction is required, and sufficient GPU memory is available [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is constrained or when immediate rasterization without intermediate storage is preferred [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of tetrahedra, as each tetrahedron is processed in parallel by a fragment shader invocation [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the size of the tetrahedral mesh, plus the overhead of four render targets (MRTs) for vertex storage [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Efficient for large datasets due to parallel fragment processing, but constrained by GPU memory capacity [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids CPU-GPU bottlenecks by keeping data on the GPU; allows direct reuse of results via render-to-vertex-arrays [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage due to MRTs and texture storage; limited by VRAM size and PCIe transfer speeds if data is updated [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When real-time or near-real-time extraction from large unstructured grids is required and sufficient GPU memory is available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When GPU memory is insufficient for the dataset or when frequent CPU-GPU data transfers are a bottleneck.
 
 ## Background
-This technique was proposed in [33] as a solution to compute isosurfaces using fragment shaders, allowing results to be reused with render-to-vertex-arrays functionality [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It builds on the framework outlined in [66] for representing mesh information in textures, but introduces a more compact representation to reduce memory footprint [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader effort to push volume rendering computations to the GPU, leveraging programmable shaders for greater flexibility and performance compared to early rasterization-based methods [S1, unofficial].
+This method was proposed in [33] as a solution to compute isosurfaces using fragment shaders, enabling results to be reused with render-to-vertex-arrays functionality [S1, S2, unofficial]. It builds on earlier work by [69] and [58], which explored rasterization hardware without programmable shaders, and [66], which outlined the framework for storing mesh information in textures [S1, unofficial]. The technique represents a shift towards fully GPU-based pipelines for volume rendering of unstructured grids, reducing reliance on CPU-side processing [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
